     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be given a mapping of ``high`` and/or ``low`` thresholds
+    instead of a bare percentage. ``high`` behaves exactly like the bare
+    percentage form above (alert when usage rises to or above the threshold).
+    ``low`` alerts when usage falls to or below the threshold, which is
+    normally a sign that the filesystem failed to mount and what looks like
+    the real disk is actually an empty directory on the root filesystem.
+    Both can be configured together for the same mount, or independently for
+    different mounts.
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                high: 50%
+                low: 5%
+            - /mnt/backups:
+                low: 5%
+
+    Beacon events triggered by a ``low`` threshold include a ``low: True``
+    field so they can be told apart from ``high``/legacy events.
+
     """
     whitelist = []
     config = salt.utils.beacons.remove_hidden_options(config, whitelist)
